<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Göttingen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Göttingen is a university town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leine">Leine</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. A member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it became famous after George II, as Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, founded its university in 1734. In 1837 seven professors, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brothers-grimm">Brothers Grimm</a>, were dismissed for protesting against King Ernest Augustus's revocation of the Hanoverian constitution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gottingen.jpg" alt="Göttingen" width="320" height="177" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Schwen · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goettingen_Marktplatz_Oct06_Antilived.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
